  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 341 AM MST Sun Nov 15 2026\n\nWarm and dry conditions are expected over the next 48 hours as \nridging returns to Wyoming. While not quite as warm as yesterday, \ntemperatures will peak 6 to 10 degrees above normal today. This will \nmake for a fairly pleasant Sunday, albeit with a gusty southwest \nwind from Farson through Natrona County induced by a pressure \ngradient set up across the state. On Monday midlevel flow becomes \nsouthwesterly as we enter the backside of the ridge ahead of the \nnext trough. Not much will change, though temperatures will increase \nslightly for most locations. \n\nThe trough will approach Tuesday as an open wave. Most guidance has \nthis digging south into the Great Basin and splitting somewhat as it \npulls northward into the Rockies. Combined with only modest \nmoisture, this does not favor a particularly noteworthy \nprecipitation event. Regardless, this will return chances to western \nWyoming beginning Tuesday morning, spreading eastward through \nTuesday night. Locations east of the Divide can expect another warm \nand windy day ahead of an associated cold front. This front will \nbring snow levels down across western Wyoming, likely to the valleys \nTuesday night. Currently total liquid amounts are under a half inch \nacross the western mountains, with less forecast in the valleys. \nForecast snow totals in the mountains are in the 3 to 5 inch range, \nfocused mostly across the Tetons, Gros Ventres, and western Wind \nRiver Range. The best chance for precipitation (rain) east of the \nDivide will be Tuesday night, though most areas are expected to \nremain dry. \n\nPrecipitation is largely expected to end by Wednesday afternoon. A \ncooler airmass will be in place, with temperatures peaking around \nmore seasonable values. Most models have the next system following \nquickly, with precipitation returning to western Wyoming beginning \nThursday. This next system appears to progress similarly to the \nformer, though perhaps a little stronger in magnitude. The current \nforecast reflects this, with more widespread precipitation chances \nfor the area from Thursday evening through Saturday morning. With \nthe colder air in place, this could bring light snow to the basins \non both sides of the Divide. While uncertainty in the progression \nand strength of this system is high, continued cooler and unsettled \nweather appears likely through the end of the week.",
